Yes, under the stated conditions. Because the partition {X_i} is data-independent and pairwise disjoint with union X, any two neighboring datasets (differing in one individual under the same neighboring relation) differ in exactly one block X_k and agree on all others. For i ≠ k, M_i(X_i) has the same distribution on both neighbors, so it contributes no privacy loss. The only contribution comes from M_k, which is (ε_k, δ_k)-DP. Hence for any neighboring pair, the joint output M(X) = (M_1(X_1), …, M_n(X_n)) is (ε_k, δ_k)-DP, and taking the worst case over which block k changes gives (max_i ε_i, max_i δ_i).

This is exactly the parallel composition statement captured by [THEOREM 7], which applies here since each M_i is DP with respect to the same neighboring relation restricted to X_i and the X_i form a data-independent partition.

\boxed{\texttt{yes}}
